Title: THE VELVETEEN RABBIT
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from the book by Margery Williams
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A velveteen rabbit -- fat and bunchy, spotted brown and white, with real thread whiskers and ears lined in pink sateen -- sits wedged in the top of a boy's Christmas stocking, a sprig of holly between his paws.

The BOY, five years old, pulls him free with shining eyes. He hugs the rabbit tight. For two hours they are inseparable.

NANA, the nursery maid, bustles in with more presents. Aunts and uncles arrive. Tissue paper rustles. New toys appear. The rabbit is dropped and forgotten on the floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a long time he lived in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor, and no one thought very much about him.

The rabbit watches the mechanical toys boast and swagger. The model boat speaks of rigging in technical terms. Even the jointed wooden lion puts on airs. The rabbit feels small and shabby.

Only the SKIN HORSE -- old, bald in patches, seams showing, tail hairs long ago pulled out for bead necklaces -- regards him with kindness.

They lie side by side near the nursery fender.

RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago. But once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The rabbit sighs, longing for the magic yet fearing the loss of his whiskers and polish.

NANA sweeps through like a wind, tidying. The rabbit tumbles softly wherever she throws him.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - EVENING

The Boy cannot find his usual china dog. Nana, in a hurry, glances at the open toy cupboard.

NANA
Here. Take your old Bunny. He'll do to sleep with you.

She drags the rabbit out by one ear and drops him into the Boy's arms.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The rabbit lies stiff at first under the Boy's tight hugs. The Boy rolls over him, pushes him under the pillow. But soon the Boy whispers to him, builds tunnels under the blankets like rabbit burrows, and plays splendid games in the dark after Nana leaves the night-light burning.

When the Boy sleeps, the rabbit snuggles under his warm chin, the small hands clasped around him all night long.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy. So happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. The Boy takes the rabbit everywhere -- in the wheelbarrow, on picnics, building fairy huts under the raspberry canes.

One evening the rabbit is left out on the lawn until after dusk. Nana finds him wet with dew and earthy from the flower-bed burrows. She rubs him with her apron, grumbling.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny. Fancy all that fuss for a toy.

The Boy sits up in bed, arms outstretched.

BOY
Give me my Bunny. You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL.

The rabbit's sawdust heart swells. He knows the Skin Horse spoke the truth. The nursery magic has happened. He is Real.

That night he lies awake, almost too happy to sleep. A look of wisdom and beauty steals into his boot-button eyes.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Long June evenings. The Boy plays at brigands among the trees and leaves the rabbit in a cosy nest of bracken.

Two wild rabbits -- furry, brand-new, able to change shape from long and thin to fat and bunchy -- creep from the tall ferns. Their noses twitch. They stare.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
It's as easy as anything.

The wild rabbit hops sideways and stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can.

RABBIT
I can jump higher than anything.

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The rabbit freezes. His back is made all in one piece like a pincushion. He has no hind legs to show.

RABBIT
I don't want to.

The wild rabbit stretches his neck, peers, then laughs.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs. Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs.

RABBIT
I have. I have got hind legs. I am sitting on them.

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this.

The wild rabbit whirls and dances. The rabbit grows dizzy with longing. A new tickly feeling runs through him. He wants to dance more than anything.

The wild rabbit comes close, whiskers brushing the rabbit's ear. He wrinkles his nose and leaps back.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right. He isn't a rabbit at all. He isn't real.

RABBIT
I am Real. I am Real. The Boy said so.

The rabbit nearly cries. Footsteps sound. The Boy runs past. With a stamp of feet and flash of white tails the wild rabbits vanish.

RABBIT
Come back and play with me. Oh, do come back. I know I am Real.

Only the ants run to and fro and the bracken sways. The rabbit is alone.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later. The rabbit is old and shabby now -- whiskers gone, ears lining turned grey, brown spots faded, shape lost. Yet to the Boy he is always beautiful.

The Boy falls ill. His face flushes. He talks in his sleep. His body burns hot. The rabbit hides under the bedclothes, afraid of being taken away. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night.

The rabbit whispers plans of the garden and raspberry thicket into the Boy's ear while he dozes.

Slowly the fever turns. The Boy improves. He sits up with picture-books. One bright morning they carry him, wrapped in a shawl, onto the balcony. The windows stand wide open.

The doctor speaks with Nana.

DOCTOR
The room is to be disinfected. All the books and toys he played with in bed must be burnt.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs. Burn it at once. Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more.

The rabbit is dropped into a sack with old picture-books and rubbish and carried out.

EXT. BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The sack lies untied in the corner where the gardener will make a bonfire tomorrow. The rabbit wriggles his head out. He shivers. His coat is threadbare. He sees the raspberry canes like a jungle, remembers the fairy huts, the wood, the ants on his paws, the day the Boy first called him Real.

A great sadness comes over him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear trickles down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

Where the tear fell, a flower grows -- slender emerald leaves, a golden cup blossom. The blossom opens. A FAIRY steps out, the loveliest in the world, dressed in pearl and dewdrops, flowers in her hair.

The fairy gathers the rabbit up, kisses his damp velveteen nose.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

RABBIT
(softly)
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to everyone.

She holds him close and flies with him into the wood.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moonlight. The bracken shines like frosted silver. Wild rabbits dance in the glade, shadows long on the velvet grass. They stop and form a ring as the Fairy appears.

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever.

She kisses the rabbit once more and sets him on the grass.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit.

The rabbit sits still, ashamed of his one-piece body. Then something tickles his nose. Without thinking he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

He has hind legs. Soft brown fur covers him, shiny and new. His ears twitch by themselves. His whiskers brush the grass. He leaps. Joy surges through him. He springs sideways, whirls, dances with the others.

When he stops, the Fairy is gone. The wild rabbits watch him with friendly eyes.

He is a Real Rabbit at last.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Spring again. The Boy, now well, plays among the trees. Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep at him. One is brown all over. The other has faint markings under his fur as though he had once been spotted. Something familiar lingers about his soft nose and round black eyes.

The Boy pauses, a half-remembered smile on his face.

BOY
(quietly)
He looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever.

The spotted rabbit looks back at the child who first made him Real, then bounds away with his companion into the wood.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The rabbit leaps high, free and real among the wild ones, disappearing into the green.

FADE OUT.

THE END
